From 2cf32731a0b174af63e0800605b1708166f7ac42 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timur Ravilov <93673987+t0b1kent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 10:14:57 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 16/19] ExitFunction: no jump to host 0 for a branch to guest 0
 (MACRUNNER_FEX_NULL_HOST)

A block exit to a non-constant target (RET, RETF, IRET, JMP/CALL r/m, also POPF and SYSCALL) takes
its host address from the call-ret stack or from the inline L1 lookup, and both match the guest
address alone. An empty entry is {guest 0, host 0}, so a branch to guest 0 (`push 0; ret`) went to
host address 0 and crashed the host (ESR 0x82000006) instead of giving the guest a page fault.
With MACRUNNER_FEX_NULL_HOST=1 (default off) a zero host candidate takes the L1 miss path, i.e. the
full lookup through the dispatcher, whose L2 lookup already rejects a zero host; a fetch at an
unmapped 0 then becomes the guest's own fault. Guest 0 itself stays allowed (it may be mapped).
Not covered: InvalidateCache keeps the HostCode of an L1 entry, so guest 0 can still match
{0, old host}; the ARM64EC entry path in Dispatcher.cpp compares the call-ret stack top without
this check.

MacRunnerNullHostChecks counts exits translated with the check; a macrunner-fex-null-host line is
printed at 1, 2, 4, ... HB<->FEX oracle (72 219 cases, product and native feature sets, one binary
for both arms, ABBA order): exactly one result changes, `push 0; ret` (host fault -> x86-correct
fetch fault at 0), no other result changes; the counter is non-zero on all 499 cases with a
non-constant exit and zero elsewhere. ARM64EC and WOW64 builds compile; not run under Wine.

